'''Photoplethysmography (PPG)''' is an optical technique that measures
volumetric blood-flow change by illuminating tissue with a
light-emitting diode (LED) and recording how much light is reflected
or transmitted back to a photodiode. Each cardiac systole pushes a
pressure wave outward; the pressure wave transiently expands the
arterioles in the illuminated tissue volume; the expanded blood
absorbs more of the LED light; the photodiode sees a periodic dip.
The resulting waveform is the PPG signal.

PPG is the standard sensing modality for [[HelmKit_Mk0.5|HelmKit Mk0.5+]]
heart-rate-variability biofeedback. It is cheap (single LED + single
photodiode + amplifier), low-power, non-invasive, and tolerates
relatively coarse mechanical mounting compared with ECG (which
requires three-electrode placement on bare skin).

== 1. Optical mechanism ==

Two wavelengths are commonly used:

* '''Green (~525 nm)''' — absorbed strongly by both
  oxyhaemoglobin and deoxyhaemoglobin; rejected by melanin to a
  manageable degree; signal-to-noise is best at the wrist and
  forehead. Used by wrist wearables (Apple Watch, Fitbit, Garmin)
  and by the [[MAX30102]]-class temple/forehead sensors HelmKit Mk0.5
  uses.
* '''Red (~660 nm) + infrared (~940 nm)''' — the classical
  pulse-oximetry pair; differential absorption of oxy- vs
  deoxyhaemoglobin lets the ratio recover SpO₂ in addition to heart
  rate. Used by fingertip clinical pulse oximeters.

For HRV-only use (no SpO₂) the green-LED variant is preferred:
better signal at the wrist and forehead, lower power, smaller die.

== 2. Signal anatomy ==

A clean PPG cycle has three named features:

# '''Systolic peak''' — maximum vascular expansion; coincides with
  the R-wave of ECG to within ~150 ms pulse-transit time.
# '''Dicrotic notch''' — brief reflux dip on the down-slope as the
  aortic valve closes.
# '''Diastolic decay''' — exponential return toward baseline as
  pressure equilibrates.

The peak-to-peak interval is the '''PPI''' (peak-to-peak interval),
the PPG analogue of the ECG R-R interval and the input to all
[[Heart_Rate_Variability_and_Psi|HRV]] metrics.

== 3. Limitations and failure modes ==

* '''Motion artifact''' — PPG is sensitive to mechanical
  perturbation; running, weightlifting, and rapid head motion can
  swamp the cardiac signal. The Mk1.5 Combat-mode build of HelmKit
  addresses this with motion-tolerant capture.
* '''Skin tone''' — green-LED PPG has documented reduced signal
  amplitude on darker skin (melanin absorption); IR-PPG is more
  robust but consumes more power.
* '''Cold extremities''' — peripheral vasoconstriction reduces
  PPG amplitude; see [[Peripheral_Vasomotor_Tone]].
* '''Respiration coupling''' — respiration modulates PPG
  amplitude at 0.1–0.5 Hz (the source of the
  [[PPG_Amplitude_Envelope|amplitude envelope]] used as a vasomotor
  proxy).

== 4. PPG vs ECG for HRV ==

ECG measures cardiac electrical depolarisation directly; PPG measures
the downstream mechanical pulse. The two differ by the
'''pulse-transit time''' (PTT), typically 150–300 ms, which itself
varies with blood pressure and vascular tone. For frequency-domain HRV
this is negligible; for sub-millisecond timing analyses (e.g. some
ψ-task protocols requiring R-wave-locked stimulus delivery) ECG is
preferred.

